\begin_layout Section
Post-hoc explanation 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Online-offline_regression"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GP can also be used to search for interpretable post-hoc formulas that represent
 the behaviour of a black box.
 Data with transition samples are created online by the black box policy
 on the real benchmark on which a symbolic regression with GP is performed.
 Due to the far lower complexity, these are subject to losses but can be
 interpreted a lot better.
 How severe these losses are depend on many factors.
 As Hein has found out in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "p. 125"
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, policies that have been trained on offline data generated by a neuropolicy
 are almost always significantly worse than both the neuropolicy and gp
 solutions that were trained on real system dynamics.
 Surrogate models are nevertheless useful as they can be evaluated cost-effectiv
ely and in parallel, but especially with autopilots or industrial controllers,
 the risk of gross misexploration, which could even destroy the benchmark
 itself, is reduced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Trusted Domain Expert
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the pinnacle of explainable intelligent approaches, human-written approaches
 represent.
 In many areas, it is therefore up to domain experts to find solution strategies.
 The main reasons to trust an expert over a machine learning approach are
 important to consider for achieving better explainability, because humans
 also have a long learning process behind them before they can be considered
 experts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The human brain, with almost 90 billion neurons, is not only far more complex
 than artificial neural networks, the architecture has been perfected in
 many areas in an evolutionary process lasting millions of years.
 This enables humans to learn a wide range of skills in a very diverse learning
 process, while being severely limited in time and exploration compared
 to machines.
 Image recognition, for example, is perfected in early childhood to such
 an extent that it hardly needs any explanation.
 The learning process is intrinsically continuously monitored by primal
 fears and drives, dangers are analysed and the learned models are thus
 endowed with a high degree of security.
 Humans are also as lazy as they can be and, instead of making an effort
 to find new concepts for a problem, they try to solve a problem with what
 they have already learned.
 Language, as well as the ability to speak, is learned as a child and at
 the same time describes everything that a person can understand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
(Ludwig Wittgenstein, 
