\begin_layout Standard
Interpersonal communication and the ability to implement successful models
 in all people (such as the Pythagorean theorem) also avoids over- and underfitt
ing as much as possible.
 As a communication protocol, a language works because the experiences between
 people are similar enough to be learned and comparable.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let's consider, for example, the game Puzzle as an NP-hard problem, where
 a whole picture has to be assembled from a pile of pieces.
 Most people know how to recognize colors, that you first have to turn puzzle
 pieces to the colored side and that you should use the picture on the box
 as a reference point.
 A popular tip for beginners is therefore that you should start with the
 edge of the puzzle.
 This is helpful, because the learning process in humans usually consists
 of the composition of many comprehensibly strung together steps.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An artificial neural network has to do without all these properties as far
 as possible.
 Through an adapted network architecture and the enormous computing power,
 a specialist idiot can nevertheless be trained to master the given task.
 In contrast to humans, a neural network is dependent on random exploration,
 which can find bias-free policies, but represents a tradeoff to the familiar
 learning process of humans.
 Solutions that are searched for with the aim of minimal representation
 in a language space as defined above can therefore be interpreted by humans,
 but since the search process is also based on chance, the formulas found
 are also free of human bias - but they still often appear to the observer
 more as a random combination of various operators than as a logically abstracte
d solution to a problem.
 
